<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Edmund Allenby</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1861">1861</a>–<a href="/years/1936">1936</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Edmund Allenby, 1st Viscount Allenby</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Edmund Allenby, 1st Viscount Allenby, was a British cavalry officer and field marshal. Commanding the Egyptian Expeditionary Force from 1917, he took Beersheba and Gaza and entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> in December 1917, then routed the Ottoman armies at Megiddo in September 1918 and occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a>. From 1919 to 1925 he served as High Commissioner for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/edmund-allenby.jpg" alt="Edmund Allenby" width="320" height="448" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Edmund_Allenby.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Edmund Allenby was born on 23 April 1861, the son of Hynman Allenby, who owned 2,000 acres in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norfolk">Norfolk</a>, and Catherine Anne Cane, and was educated at Haileybury College. He had little desire to be a soldier and failed the examination for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-civil-service">Indian Civil Service</a>, but passed that for Sandhurst in 1880 and was commissioned in the 6th (Inniskilling) Dragoons on 10 May 1882.</p><p>He served in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warren-expedition">Bechuanaland Expedition</a> of 1884–85 and in the Second Boer War, in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herbert-kitchener">Lord Kitchener</a> described him as a popular and capable cavalry brigadier. A tendency to sudden bellowing rages at his subordinates, combined with his powerful frame, earned him the nickname The Bull.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he commanded the cavalry division of the British Expeditionary Force, covering the retreat after the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mons">Mons</a> and fighting at the First Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ypres">Ypres</a>. He led the Cavalry Corps from 1914, and in May 1915 took command of V Corps at the Second Ypres, where his policy of continual counterattacks defeated the German assault but at controversially heavy cost. In May 1917 the War Cabinet was divided over resources for the Western Front and other fronts, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-lloyd-george">David Lloyd George</a> appointed Allenby to replace Sir Archibald Murray in command of the Egyptian Expeditionary Force after <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jan-smuts">Jan Smuts</a> refused the post.</p><p>From October to December 1917 his forces captured Beersheba, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaffa">Jaffa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and in the summer of 1918 they occupied the Jordan Valley. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-megiddo">Battle of Megiddo</a> they defeated the Ottoman Eighth Army, and the Desert Mounted Corps then captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and advanced into northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a>. T. E. Lawrence and Faisal's forces took part in the pursuit, and the Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aleppo">Aleppo</a> was fought five days before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armistice-of-mudros">Armistice of Mudros</a> ended the campaign on 30 October 1918.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Made a field marshal on 31 July 1919, Allenby became Special High Commissioner of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> that year, as demonstrations against British rule disrupted the country and leaders such as Saad Zaghlul had been exiled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malta">Malta</a>. He first took a conciliatory line, persuading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/colonial-office">Colonial Office</a> to let Zaghlul's delegation travel to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and as High Commissioner he favoured negotiations, after which the Milner Mission was begun.</p><p>He remained a hated figure in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>, and at Port Said the burning of an effigy of him became a spring tradition. His only child, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/horace">Horace</a>, was killed in action in 1917. Allenby died of a ruptured cerebral aneurysm on 14 May 1936 at his house in Kensington, and his ashes were buried in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Edmund Allenby, 1st Viscount Allenby” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After General <b>Allenby</b>'s forces took Jerusalem in December 1917 and conquered the rest of the Levant in 1918, the occupied Ottoman territories were placed under military administration as Occupied Enemy Territory, as international law required until a peace settlement.</blockquote><a href="/state/occupied-enemy-territory-administration">Occupied Enemy Territory Administration</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nottinghamshire">Nottinghamshire</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/united-kingdom">the United Kingdom</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=53.17,-1&amp;year=1909" aria-label="Nottinghamshire on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Edmund Allenby"><image href="/maps/globes/g000n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4949 5583h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4949 5583h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=53.17,-1&amp;year=1909">What was here in 1909? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
